
1. Where you stand right now

Here is an honest picture of your current skills and what each one means to a recruiter.

Skill	Your level (likely)	What it means for placement
Python	Comfortable with basics	Good start. You still need OOP, exceptions, file handling, modules and clean code habits to clear technical rounds.
NumPy, Pandas	Can clean and analyse data	Useful for data analyst and ML roles. Helpful in projects, but rarely asked deeply in fresher interviews.
Matplotlib, Seaborn	Can make charts	Nice to have. Becomes valuable only when you use it to tell a story from real data.
FastAPI	Learning now	This is your strongest bet. Backend developers with Python and FastAPI are in demand at startups and product companies.
DSA	Probably not started	Biggest gap. Almost every company has a coding round. Start now.
SQL and databases	Probably basic	Second biggest gap. Asked in nearly every technical interview, for every role.
Git and GitHub	Unknown	Must have. Your GitHub becomes your proof of work.
Core subjects (OOP, DBMS, OS, CN)	From college syllabus	Asked heavily in service company interviews. Revise with interview focus.

So the gaps to close, in order of importance, are: DSA, SQL, finishing FastAPI properly with a database and authentication, building and deploying real projects, and preparing core subjects and aptitude for the campus tests.

2. Choose your direction

Without a clear target role you will keep learning random things. Here are the three roles that fit your current skills.

Role 1: Python Backend Developer

• Core skills needed: Python, FastAPI or Django, SQL, PostgreSQL, REST APIs, Git, Docker basics, testing.
• Who hires freshers: Startups, product companies, service companies.
• Fit for you: Best fit. You are already on this path.

Role 2: Data Analyst

• Core skills needed: SQL (very strong), Excel, Power BI or Tableau, Pandas, statistics, storytelling.
• Who hires freshers: Service companies, analytics firms, banks, startups.
• Fit for you: Strong backup. You already have half the skills.

Role 3: AI or ML Engineer (fresher)

• Core skills needed: Python, ML basics, Scikit Learn, LLM APIs, RAG, FastAPI to serve models.
• Who hires freshers: Fewer fresher openings, mostly startups.
• Fit for you: Add as a specialisation on top of backend, not as the only plan.
My recommendation: make Python Backend Developer your main target, keep Data Analyst as your backup, and add one solid AI project on top. This combination is powerful in 2026, because almost every AI product needs a backend person who can build the API, store the data and connect the model. You get three doors open with one set of skills.
When would I change this advice? If you genuinely enjoy finding insights in data far more than building systems, flip it: make Data Analyst the main target and learn Power BI and advanced SQL first. Decide within your first month and then stop switching.

3. The big picture timeline

MCA is two years. In most colleges, campus placement drives begin in the third semester, which means you realistically have around eight to ten months before companies start visiting. Check with your placement cell for your college's exact schedule and adjust the plan below.

Phase	When	Focus	What you should have at the end
Phase 1: Foundation	November to January	Git and GitHub, core Python, DSA basics, SQL basics, finish FastAPI fundamentals	GitHub profile live, 60 DSA problems, 30 SQL problems, small FastAPI app.
Phase 2: Build	February to April	Project 1 and Project 2, PostgreSQL, authentication, DSA medium level	Two projects on GitHub with good READMEs, one deployed, 140 DSA problems.
Phase 3: Level up	May to July	Project 3 and Project 4, Docker, testing, internship applications, core CS subjects	Four projects, an internship or freelance work if possible, 200 DSA problems.
Phase 4: Placement ready	August onwards	Capstone AI project, aptitude, mock interviews, resume, applying everywhere	Five projects, 250 plus problems, a sharp one page resume, interview confidence.

Your semester exams will interrupt this plan. That is normal. During exam weeks, keep only one thing alive: solve one DSA problem a day and push it to GitHub. Do not let the chain break completely.

4. Step by step: what to learn and where to practise


Step 1: Git and GitHub (week 1)

Do this first because every other step will be saved on GitHub. Learn the commands you will use daily: init, clone, add, commit, push, pull, branch, merge, and how to write a .gitignore file. Understand what a pull request is.
• Learn: GitHub Skills (://github.com) for guided practice, and Learn Git Branching (learngitbranching.js.org) to visually understand branches.
• Practise: create your GitHub account with a professional username (your real name works best), add a photo and bio, and create your profile README.
• Done when: you can create a repo, push code from your laptop, make a branch, merge it, and fix a simple merge conflict without searching every step.

Step 2: Strengthen core Python (weeks 1 to 4)

Many students who know Pandas are surprisingly weak in plain Python. Interviewers test plain Python. Cover these topics properly: data types and their methods, list and dictionary comprehensions, functions and arguments, OOP (classes, inheritance, polymorphism, encapsulation, dunder methods), exception handling, file handling, modules and packages, virtual environments, decorators, generators, iterators, and type hints. Type hints matter a lot because FastAPI is built on them.
• Learn: CS50P, Harvard's free Python course (cs50.harvard.edu/python), Real Python articles (realpython.com), the official tutorial (docs.python.org).
• Practise: HackerRank Python track (hackerrank.com), and Python Tutor (pythontutor.com) whenever you cannot picture what your code is doing.
• GitHub: repo named python_core_practice with one folder per topic. Every topic gets small programs plus a short notes.md in your own words.
• Done when: you can write a small class based program, for example a library management system in the terminal, without copying code.

Step 3: Data Structures and Algorithms (start now, continue till placement)

This is the step most students delay, and it is the one that decides whether you clear the first round. Use Python for DSA, since it is your strongest language and it is accepted almost everywhere.
Follow this order. Do not jump around.
1. Time and space complexity (Big O). Learn this first, otherwise you cannot judge your own solutions.
2. Arrays and strings, including two pointers and sliding window.
3. Hashing using dictionaries and sets.
4. Sorting and binary search.
5. Recursion and backtracking basics.
6. Stacks and queues.
7. Linked lists.
8. Trees and binary search trees.
9. Heaps (Python's heapq).
10. Graphs: BFS, DFS, and basic shortest path.
11. Dynamic programming basics: start with 1D problems.
• Learn: Striver's A2Z DSA course and sheet (takeuforward.org), NeetCode roadmap and videos (neetcode.io), GeeksforGeeks articles (geeksforgeeks.org).
• Practise: LeetCode (leetcode.com) as the main platform. Use GeeksforGeeks practice and CodeChef (codechef.com) if you want extra easy problems or contests.
• Target: 250 to 300 well understood problems by placement time. Roughly 40 percent easy, 50 percent medium, 10 percent hard. Quality matters more than count.
• GitHub: repo named dsa_in_python, organised by topic folders. Each solution file should have the problem link, your approach in two or three lines, and the time and space complexity as comments.
• Daily rule: minimum one problem every day. On weekends, attempt a LeetCode contest even if you solve only one question.
A simple method that works: try a problem for 30 to 40 minutes. If stuck, read only the hint or approach, not the code. Write the code yourself. Then revisit the same problem after one week without looking. If you cannot solve it again, you did not really learn it.

Step 4: SQL and databases (weeks 3 to 10)

SQL is asked in nearly every interview, and for backend and data roles it is absolutely essential. Learn it with PostgreSQL, because that is what you will use with FastAPI.
• Topics: SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT, aggregate functions, GROUP BY and HAVING, all types of JOINs, subqueries, CTEs (WITH clause), window functions (ROW_NUMBER, RANK, LAG, LEAD), CASE WHEN, constraints and keys, indexes, normalization up to 3NF, transactions and ACID properties.
• Learn: SQLBolt (sqlbolt.com) for the basics in a day or two, then PostgreSQL Tutorial (postgresqltutorial.com).
• Practise: the LeetCode SQL 50 study plan on leetcode.com, HackerRank SQL track, and SQLZoo (sqlzoo.net).
• GitHub: repo named sql_practice with one .sql file per topic, plus a small database design of your own (for example a college library) with a diagram in the README.
• Done when: you can find the second highest salary in each department, explain the difference between WHERE and HAVING, and design tables for a simple app without help.

Step 5: FastAPI, done properly (weeks 4 to 16)

Most students stop at making a few endpoints that return JSON. To be job ready you must go further. Learn it in this order:
1. Path and query parameters, request body, response models.
2. Pydantic models and validation in depth.
3. Routers and project structure (do not keep everything in main.py).
4. Connecting to a database using SQLAlchemy, first SQLite and then PostgreSQL.
5. Database migrations with Alembic.
6. Dependency injection (the Depends system), one of FastAPI's best features.
7. Authentication with password hashing and JWT tokens, then role based authorization.
8. Error handling, status codes, and logging.
9. Background tasks and file uploads.
10. Testing with pytest and FastAPI's TestClient.
11. Environment variables and settings management, never hard coding secrets.
12. Docker, so your app runs the same everywhere, then deployment.
• Learn: the official FastAPI tutorial (://tiangolo.com) is excellent, read it fully. Also the SQLAlchemy docs (docs.sqlalchemy.org), Pydantic docs (docs.pydantic.dev), pytest docs (docs.pytest.org), and Docker's getting started guide (://docker.com).
• Tools: Postman (postman.com) or the built in /docs page FastAPI gives you for testing APIs.
• GitHub: repo named fastapi_learning where each concept is a small numbered folder. Your real projects get their own separate repos.

Step 6: Core computer science subjects (from month 5)

Service companies (TCS, Infosys, Wipro, Cognizant, Accenture, Capgemini and similar) ask these a lot, and product companies ask them too. Revise with an interview mindset, not an exam mindset.

Subject	Most asked topics	Where to learn
OOP	Four pillars, method overloading vs overriding, abstract classes, interfaces, real life examples	GeeksforGeeks, your own Python practice.
DBMS	Keys, normalization, ACID, transactions, indexing, joins, ER diagrams	Gate Smashers on YouTube, GeeksforGeeks.
Operating Systems	Process vs thread, scheduling, deadlock, paging, virtual memory, semaphores	Gate Smashers or Neso Academy on YouTube.
Computer Networks	OSI and TCP/IP models, HTTP and HTTPS, DNS, what happens when you type a URL, TCP vs UDP	Neso Academy, GeeksforGeeks.

Keep one Google Doc or Markdown file per subject with your own short notes. Revise those notes in the month before placements instead of rewatching full playlists.

Step 7: Machine learning basics (from month 6, optional but recommended)

Since you already know Pandas and visualisation, adding basic ML is a short step and supports both your backup role and your AI project. Learn: train and test split, linear and logistic regression, decision trees, random forest, KMeans clustering, evaluation metrics (accuracy, precision, recall, F1, confusion matrix), and overfitting. Use the Scikit Learn library.
• Learn: Kaggle Learn courses (://kaggle.com), free and hands on. The Scikit Learn official user guide for reference.
• Practise: Kaggle datasets and beginner competitions such as Titanic.
• Then: learn how LLM based apps work: embeddings, vector search, and RAG. Hugging Face (huggingface.co) has free courses and models.

Step 8: Aptitude, reasoning and communication (from month 7)

Many campus drives start with an online aptitude test, and good coders get rejected here simply because they never practised. Spend 30 minutes a day on it in the last four to five months.
• Topics: percentages, profit and loss, time and work, speed and distance, ratios, probability, permutations, number series, coding and decoding, blood relations, seating arrangements, reading comprehension, grammar.
• Practise: IndiaBix (indiabix.com) and PrepInsta (prepinsta.com), which also has company specific previous patterns.
• Communication: explain one concept out loud every day for two minutes, in English, as if teaching a junior. Record it on your phone and listen once. It feels awkward, and it works.

Step 9: Resume, LinkedIn and applying (from month 8)

• Resume: one page only. Use a clean template, for example Jake's resume template on Overleaf (overleaf.com). Order: education, skills, projects, internships, achievements. Every project line should show what you built, the stack, and one result or number.
• LinkedIn: clear photo, headline like "MCA student | Python Backend Developer | FastAPI, PostgreSQL", and post when you finish each project with a short demo video.
• Apply beyond campus: Internshala (internshala.com), Unstop (unstop.com), Naukri (naukri.com), LinkedIn Jobs, Wellfound (wellfound.com) for startups, and Instahyre (instahyre.com).
• Internship: try hard to get one internship or a real freelance project during Phase 3. Even a small paid or unpaid one with a startup changes how interviewers see you.

5. The GitHub practice system

Every single thing you practise goes on GitHub. Not because a green contribution graph impresses anyone by itself, but because it forces you to finish things, write clearly about them, and build a public record that a recruiter can open in thirty seconds.

Repositories to create


Repository name	What goes inside
your_username (profile README)	A short introduction, your target role, tech stack, current focus, and links to your best projects and LinkedIn.
python_core_practice	Folders by topic: oop, exceptions, file_handling, decorators and so on, each with code and notes.md.
dsa_in_python	Folders by topic: arrays, strings, hashing, trees, graphs, dp. One file per problem with link, approach and complexity.
sql_practice	One .sql file per topic plus your own database design project.
fastapi_learning	Small numbered folders, one concept each, built while following the docs.
Project repositories	One separate repo for each of the five projects in Section 6, each with a proper README.

Use underscores in repository names, keep them lowercase, and keep names meaningful. A name like test123 or my_project_final tells the reader nothing.

Rules that make your GitHub look professional

1. Commit every day you study, even if it is one DSA solution. Never create fake commits or copy someone else's repository. Interviewers do ask you to explain your code line by line.
2. Write clear commit messages that describe what changed, for example: "Add JWT login endpoint", "Fix total calculation for monthly report", "Write tests for expense routes". Avoid messages like "update" or "final".
3. Never push passwords, API keys or .env files. Add them to .gitignore and share a .env.example file with empty values instead.
4. Every project repo must have a README with: project title and one line description, a screenshot or GIF, the live link, features, tech stack, how to run it locally, API endpoint list, what you learned, and future improvements.
5. Pin your best four to six repositories on your profile, with your strongest project first.
6. Once you are comfortable, use branches for new features and merge through pull requests, even when working alone. It shows you understand real team workflow.
7. In Phase 3, add GitHub Actions to run your tests automatically on every push. It is a small effort and very few freshers do it.

