How to build it, step by step

1. Write down all the endpoints on paper first, with method, path and what each returns.
2. Design two tables, users and expenses, with a foreign key from expenses to users.
3. Set up the project, virtual environment, and the folder structure above. Push this skeleton to GitHub on day one.
4. Build the expense CRUD without login first, test everything in the /docs page.
5. Add registration and login, then protect the expense routes so each user only sees their own records.
6. Add filters and the summary endpoint. This is where your SQL GROUP BY knowledge gets used.
7. Write tests for the main routes. Aim for at least ten meaningful tests.
8. Move from SQLite to PostgreSQL and add Alembic migrations.
9. Write the README with endpoint list and screenshots, then deploy.

Deployment

Deploy the API on Render (render.com) or Railway (railway.com) and use a free PostgreSQL database from a provider such as Supabase (supabase.com) or Neon. Free plans and their limits change often, so check the current terms before you choose. Put the live /docs link in your README.

Questions this project prepares you for

• What is REST and what makes an API RESTful?
• How does JWT authentication work, and where is the token stored?
• Why should passwords be hashed and not encrypted?
• What is the difference between PUT and PATCH?
• How did you make sure one user cannot see another user's data?

Project 2: Sales Insights Dashboard

What it is: An interactive dashboard built on a real public dataset, for example a retail or online shopping sales dataset from Kaggle. It answers real business questions: which products and regions sell most, how sales change by month, who the best customers are, and what should the business do next.
Why it impresses: It turns your existing Pandas, Matplotlib and Seaborn knowledge into something a recruiter can actually open and use. It also directly supports your Data Analyst backup path. The difference between a beginner and a strong candidate here is the written insights, not the number of charts.

Tech stack

• Data: A public sales dataset from Kaggle (://kaggle.com)
• Analysis: Pandas and NumPy, in a Jupyter notebook first
• Charts: Matplotlib and Seaborn in the notebook, Plotly for interactive charts in the app
• App: Streamlit (streamlit.io)
• Storage: CSV at first, then load the cleaned data into PostgreSQL and query it with SQL

Must have features

• A cleaning notebook that documents every step: missing values, duplicates, wrong data types, outliers.
• KPI cards: total revenue, total orders, average order value, number of customers.
• Monthly trend, top ten products, sales by region and category.
• Filters by date range, region and category.
• A written insights section with five to seven findings and a recommendation for each.

Extra features once the basics work

• Customer segmentation using RFM analysis (recency, frequency, monetary).
• A simple sales forecast for the next three months.
• Rebuild the same dashboard in Power BI and compare. Useful if you lean towards the analyst role.

