Weekly review questions

• What did I complete this week? Is it on GitHub?
• What did I plan but not do, and what was the real reason?
• How many DSA problems did I solve, and how many could I solve again without help?
• What is the single most important thing for next week?
Rest one evening a week. Burnout in month four will cost you more than one evening off every week.

9. Interview preparation


How to explain any project

Interviewers spend most of the time on your projects. Prepare every project using this five part structure, and practise saying it out loud in about two minutes:
1. What: what the project does in one or two sentences.
2. Why: the problem it solves and why you chose to build it.
3. How: the architecture, the stack, and how data flows through it.
4. Trade offs: decisions you made and what you gave up. For example, why PostgreSQL instead of MongoDB.
5. Challenge: one real bug or difficulty you faced and how you solved it.

How to handle the coding round

1. Understand the problem fully. Repeat it in your own words and ask about edge cases.
2. Think aloud. Explain the simple brute force idea first, then improve it.
3. Write clean code with meaningful variable names.
4. Test your code by walking through an example, including an edge case such as an empty list.
5. State the time and space complexity without being asked.

HR round

Prepare honest answers for: tell me about yourself, why this company, your strengths and weaknesses, a time you failed, where you see yourself in three years, and why MCA. Write them down once, then practise speaking them naturally rather than memorising word for word.

Practise with people

Do mock interviews with classmates every week in Phase 4. One person asks, one answers, then switch. Ask seniors who got placed to take one mock interview for you. It is uncomfortable, and that discomfort is exactly what you want to get used to before the real day.

10. Mistakes I see students make again and again

• Tutorial loop: Watching course after course without building. Rule: for every hour of video, spend at least two hours writing your own code.
• Learning too many things: Django, Flask, FastAPI, React, Java and Flutter at the same time. Stick to the stack in this guide until you are placed.
• Copying projects: Cloning a project from YouTube and putting it on your resume. Interviewers can tell within three questions.
• Leaving DSA for later: DSA cannot be crammed in the last month. Start today, even with one easy problem.
• Ignoring aptitude: Strong coders get filtered out in the first online test. Do not let that be you.
• Unfinished projects: Three finished and deployed projects beat eight unfinished ones every time.
• Comparing with others: Someone will always seem ahead. Compare yourself only with who you were last month.
• Relying on AI to write your code: Use AI tools to understand concepts and debug, but write the code yourself. If you cannot explain a line, you do not own it yet.

11. Final checklist before placement season

Go through this list about one month before drives begin. Every item should honestly be a yes.
• DSA: 250 or more problems solved, and you can solve a medium level problem in about 30 minutes.
• SQL: Comfortable with joins, GROUP BY, subqueries and window functions.
• Python: Can explain OOP concepts with your own code examples.
• FastAPI: Can build a CRUD API with database and JWT authentication from scratch without a tutorial.
• Projects: At least four complete projects, at least three deployed, all with proper READMEs.
• GitHub: Profile README done, best repos pinned, consistent activity over months.
• Core CS: Short notes for OOP, DBMS, OS and CN revised at least twice.
• Aptitude: Scoring well and consistently on PrepInsta or IndiaBix mock tests.
• Resume: One page, reviewed by a senior or teacher, every line backed by real work.
• LinkedIn: Complete profile, a few posts about your projects.
• Interviews: At least five mock interviews done, project explanations practised out loud.

A last word

Placement is not a single exam that you pass or fail. It is the result of many small days of work added together. If you follow this guide even seventy percent of the way, you will be ahead of most students in your batch. Start today with something small: create your GitHub account, push your first file, and solve one easy problem. Then do it again tomorrow.
All the best. Keep building.
